      name: "Meghalaya",
      summary: "Meghalaya's Khasi, Jaintia and Garo peoples carry their knowledge orally; Khasi folklore itself explains the absence of writing through the legend of a script lost in a great flood. Writing arrived with the Welsh missionary Thomas Jones's Latin-script Khasi in 1841, and the Seng Khasi movement (founded 1899) has since institutionalized the defense of the indigenous Niam Khasi religion and its oral lore.",
      vedicConnection: "There is no Vedic connection: the Khasi-Jaintia hills lay entirely outside Vedic composition and shakha transmission, and their authentic tradition is oral. A well-known Khasi folktale holds that the Khasi lost their script while swimming a flooded river — the scroll was swallowed with the water — an origin story for a purely oral culture whose ritual, cosmological and customary knowledge passed by word of mouth. The Seng Khasi, founded by sixteen young men at Mawkhar, Shillong in 1899 under the guidance of Babu Jeebon Roy, preserves Niam Khasi religion, lore and custom against assimilation.",
