      summary: "Mizo knowledge was historically oral, transmitted through song, chant and the zawlbuk — the village bachelors' dormitory where young men were trained in hunting, warfare and community values. The Mizo had no writing until the missionaries J. H. Lorrain and F. W. Savidge devised a Roman-script alphabet in 1894, after which the oral corpus rapidly entered print and literacy spread through mission schools.",
